l  an even operator this is extremely  important because it means that when we  have an even potential  we already know what sort of solutions  to expect before we do any maths  the energy eigenstates have to be either  even or odd states  such insight can be really helpful in  simplifying the solution  of problems in quantum mechanics  to finish i just want to give you two  examples of even potentials to  demonstrate that indeed their  eigenstates have a definite parity  we'll work in the position  representation in terms of wave  functions because it is very easy to  think about position dependent  potentials in the position basis  the first one is the infinite square  well potential  and for full details you can find our  video on this potential link in the  description  let's first set up a pair of coordinate  axes  is a diagram of the potential which is 0  between minus a over 2 and a over 2  and infinite outside  mathematically we can write it like this  you can very clearly see from both the  diagram and the mathematical expression  that the potential is described by an  even function  so the overall hamiltonian is even we  then expect the eigenstates to have  definite parity  and indeed in the detailed video on the  infinite square well we find that the  eigenstates  psi are labeled by positive integers n  and that they are proportional to a  cosine  for odd n and proportional to a sine  for even n outside well the wave  function vanishes  as expected we obtain states of fixed  parity even states described by a cosine  and odd state described by a sign  here i have plots for the six lowest  energy eigenstates of the infinite  square well  i'm not including dimensions on the axis  because all we want to focus on  are the parity properties of the  eigenfunctions  the first one here corresponds to the  ground state labeled by n equals 1  and is clearly an even state the second  one corresponds to n equals 2  and is clearly an odd state the n equals  3  and n equals five eigenstates are also  even states  and the n equals four and n equals six  states are odd states  before moving on to the next example i  should point out that you will very  often encounter the infinite square well  potential with a different  origin where the zero of the potential  is between zero  and a in this case the potential is  clearly not  an even function so the hamiltonian will  not be even and the eigenstate will not  have definite parity  although many textbooks use this  potential from 0 to a and you can indeed  solve it to determine the corresponding  eigen states  i think using this symmetric potential  up here is much more insightful  because it exploits the inversion  symmetry present in the system  the second and final example i want to  look at is the quantum harmonic  oscillator  and again for full details you can find  our videos on this potential link in the  description  let's set up a pair of axes again this  is the diagram of the potential  its mathematical form simply shows the  quadratic dependent on the displacement  and again you can see from both the  diagram and the mathematical expression  that the potential is described by an  even function  so the overall hamiltonian is again even  the eigen states should have definite  parity  and indeed in the detailed video on the  quantum harmonic oscillator eigenstates  we find that the eigenstates are labeled  by  psi n of x where n is a non-negative  integer  and are equal to this pre-factor  a hermite polynomial of order n  and a gaussian these functions here  the hermit polynomials are even for even  n and od for odd n this is just a  gaussian  which is an even function so overall the  eigenstates of the quantum harmonic  oscillator  are even for even n and alt for odd n  again as expected they have definite  parity  finally here i have plot for the sixth  lowest energy eigenstate of the quantum  harmonic oscillator  the eigenstates 0 2 and 4  are clearly even functions while 1  3 and 5 are odd functions  we've just seen a few examples of even  hamiltonians and their even and old  eigenstates  you can check out other systems which  also have even hamiltonians for example  the hydrogen atom and as always  if you liked the video please subscribe
 

